      "latency_ms": 2596.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 758,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, on a practical and planetary level, water is much more than a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, which fills the oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F). Found in the air as humidity and in clouds.\n\n### 2. Unique and Miraculous Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transport medium, carrying minerals, nutrients, and chemicals through living organisms and the environment.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a drink, and why lakes freeze from the top down, allowing aquatic life to survive underneath during the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which is how water moves up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite its abundance, **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes and rivers.\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through the Earth in a closed system driven by the sun. It evaporates from oceans, forms clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back to the sea.\n\n### 4. Why Water Matters for Life\nWater is the essential ingredient for life as we know it. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It lubricates joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste. Without water, human cells would cease to function within days.\n*   **Civilization:** Throughout history, human civilizations have always risen near sources of fresh water (rivers and lakes) for drinking, agriculture, and hygiene.\n\n**In short:** Water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with complex, life-giving properties that shapes our geography, our climate, and our biology."
